WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:07.590
- I'm Kim Goff-Crews,

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:11.006
and it is my privilege to
welcome all of you today

00:00:11.006 --> 00:00:13.230
to honor our veterans.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:16.530
Before we begin, I would like to thank

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.830
our ROTC cadets and midshipmen

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.660
for presenting the colors

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.480
and to express our deep gratitude

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.300
to the Quinnipiac people for
the land on which we stand.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.470
I also wanna acknowledge and
recognize our alumni leaders.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.863
The Yale Alumni Association
is in the middle of a meeting

00:00:33.863 --> 00:00:35.400
for the last couple of days.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.430
They have changed their
schedule to join us

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.180
for this special event, so welcome.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.560
But I also wanna make a special welcome,

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:47.940
and that's to the students,
the faculty, staff, and alumni

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.380
who are with us today,

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.200
who have served, who are serving,

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.063
and who are preparing to serve.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.690
We are so grateful, all
of us are so grateful,

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.340
for the freedoms and privileges

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.623
that your sacrifices have made possible.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:04.290
Now, during our ceremony,

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:08.010
you will hear from a veteran
leader and a current student,

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:10.320
and we will also honor a staff person

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:13.143
who has supported and
advocated for Yale veterans.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.950
Our speakers and honorees
represent commitment

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.770
to honor and country, to our veterans,

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.533
and to those who care
for and support them.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.190
Before we begin, it's
my pleasure to introduce

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.580
President Peter Salovey,
who's gonna share his thoughts

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.470
about our esteemed veterans.

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:32.940
President Salovey.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.107
(audience applauding)

00:01:43.006 --> 00:01:46.673
- Thank you, Kim, and
good morning everyone.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.730
I'm really thrilled to
see so many students,

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.890
faculty members, staff,

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:56.070
and especially our alumni with us today

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.320
as we commemorate Veterans Day,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.800
and I wish to begin

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.390
by extending a special warm welcome

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:07.140
to the veterans and Yale ROTC students

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:10.530
whom we are honored to have here with us.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.690
You have our deepest gratitude and respect

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:14.853
for your contributions to our country,

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.060
for exemplifying a spirit of service

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.170
which dates to the
founding of our institution

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.570
and of the republic.

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:29.340
Indeed, the call to serve
society was inscribed

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.200
in this university's 1701 charter,

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.770
and this call has been answered dutifully

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:42.390
and with distinction by
generations of Yale students,

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:46.620
faculty, staff, and alumni

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.063
for over three centuries.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.420
We think, for instance, of Nathan Hale,

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.700
perhaps the most famous Yale alumnus

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.103
to serve in the American Revolution,

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.520
who perished with the lone lament

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.693
that he had but one life
to lose for his country.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.160
We think too of the nearly 30,000 Yalies

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.980
who served in the First
and Second World Wars,

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.470
Korea, and Vietnam,

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:15.630
and service members up to the present,

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:18.990
including those who you
see all around us today

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:22.383
who are carrying forward
a proud tradition.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.490
We also reflect with reverence

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.350
on the service of those veterans

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.820
who were denied freedom

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:34.080
even as they fought
valiantly for its cause.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.340
Patriots who,

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:39.937
as Dr. Cortlandt Van
Rensselaer Creed exclaimed,

00:03:39.937 --> 00:03:44.937
"Still rallied to the sound
of liberty and union."

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.200
Dr. Creed, the first African American

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.720
to earn a medical degree at Yale,

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:56.130
served honorably in the Union
Army during the Civil War

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:59.613
in spite of tremendous
racism and discrimination.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.630
Closer to our time,

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:07.950
patriots like Yale College
alumnus and my friend,

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:12.270
retired Lieutenant Colonel
Enoch Woodhouse, Woody,

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:15.438
one of the last surviving Tuskegee Airmen,

00:04:15.438 --> 00:04:20.253
endured segregated conditions
in defense of this country.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.730
I also think of Admiral Grace Hopper

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.610
who earned her PhD at Yale

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.400
and is the namesake of one
of our residential colleges.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:34.400
She led in fields that were at
the time unwelcome to women.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.473
And now in our era,

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:42.210
we are resolved to build as best we can

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.283
on their enduring legacies.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:48.870
Yale's Reserve Officers
Training Corps program,

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.670
for instance, was recently lauded

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:54.780
by U.S. Secretary of Defense Lloyd Austin

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:58.710
for increasing diversity through
the officers it produces,

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:02.370
including in areas of
the active duty force

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.803
that are underrepresented by women.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:10.020
Yale Law School meanwhile
is at the fore of the effort

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:14.613
to rid the veterans benefit
system of racial disparities.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:18.480
And earlier this semester,

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:21.820
the U.S. News and World
Report recognized Yale

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:25.690
as the best college in
the nation for veterans

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.950
in part due to our commitment

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:33.950
to ensuring that a Yale education
is affordable to everyone.

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:36.690
I don't love those rankings,

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:39.095
but I do love it when we're number one.

00:05:39.095 --> 00:05:41.160
(audience laughing)

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:45.900
Of course, our tradition
of service continues

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:47.490
through the students

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:51.120
who will one day join the eminent ranks

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.493
of the Yale veterans community.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.780
The ROTC program on campus recently marked

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.413
the 10th anniversary of its return,

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:05.430
and the spirit of
sacrifice that links these

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:07.170
and future military members

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:11.613
is a hallmark of the
spirit of Yale itself.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.500
It is the desire to ensure
the security and welfare

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.563
of this and future generations.

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:24.633
It is the desire to serve,
to look beyond self-interest.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:30.710
It is in short the desire to
contribute to the common good.

00:06:32.474 --> 00:06:36.150
I'm deeply grateful for the
veterans here with us today

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:40.410
who have given of themselves
to a better world,

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:45.270
and for the cadets and
midshipmen of our ROTC program

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:50.270
who have similarly felt and
answered the call to duty.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:54.840
Your decision to serve in the armed forces

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:56.190
is especially critical

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:59.400
during this period of global turbulence.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:04.400
Conflicts are escalating,
democracies are backsliding,

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:09.120
and right now the spirit of
division around the world

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.253
seems stronger than ever.

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:17.390
Yet today, we look around this room

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:18.790
and this community

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:25.340
and what we see is the spirit of service.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:28.413
It's a spirit forged over centuries.

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:31.230
Indeed, the fabric of our nation

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:34.180
is woven by the individuals
who have defended it

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.293
for nearly 250 years.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:41.430
The resilience and wisdom of our veterans

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:42.993
help to build a nation,

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:47.310
and their strength help
to bind it together

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:48.933
during difficult times.

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:55.083
Their courage helped save a
world torn apart by war twice.

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:58.860
Their determination has protected millions

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.013
from a life of oppression and hardship.

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:06.240
And as recent years have underscored,

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:09.840
their compassion and
commitment to humanity

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:14.840
have brought food, clothing,
and shelter, medication

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:17.673
to those in need of it most.

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.210
The exigent crises we face as
a country and global community

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:28.173
remind us anew of the fragility of peace,

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.910
but they also redouble our gratitude

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:37.503
for those who have answered
the call to protect it.

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:41.490
In both peace time and wartime,

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:44.580
Yalies have answered this call to duty,

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:48.573
and many have made the ultimate
sacrifice for their country.

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:53.460
Carved into the hallowed
walls of Memorial Hall

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:55.650
right outside those doors,

00:08:55.650 --> 00:09:00.650
you can find the names of 1,020
Yale alumni killed in battle

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:03.423
since the American Revolution.

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.800
Out on Hewitt Quadrangle,

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.800
the memorial cenotaph honors
225 Yale students and alumni

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:15.153
who died in the Great War.

00:09:16.786 --> 00:09:20.910
And the Ledyard flagstaff
is dedicated to the memory

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:23.100
of a young Yale alumnus

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:26.313
who died during the
Philippine-American war.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:33.030
Today, we assemble in the
shadow of these campus landmarks

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:38.030
and in awe of the valor
to which they pay tribute

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:43.533
and of the veterans who gave
their loyalty to this nation.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:48.300
I join a grateful university community

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:52.870
in saluting those who have
so nobly served this country

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:58.880
and advanced the cause of
peace around the world.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:00.665
Thank you all very much.

00:10:00.665 --> 00:10:03.832
(audience applauding)

00:10:17.584 --> 00:10:22.584
("The Star-Spangled Banner")
- To a-ten-hut!

00:10:47.567 --> 00:10:52.150
("The Star-Spangled Banner" continues)

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:22.233
("The Star-Spangled Banner" continues)

00:11:32.243 --> 00:11:35.125
(indistinct) rest!

00:11:35.125 --> 00:11:36.958
- [Soldier] Order. Go!

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:59.300
- Grand architect of the
universe, called by many names,

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:02.373
in many languages, by many voices.

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:07.620
Today, we ask that your
guiding hands hold us

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:11.883
and all sisters and brothers
in military service near.

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:19.670
We are grateful to all who now
serve, all who have served,

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:21.540
and for those who,

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:24.277
in the words of President Abraham Lincoln,

00:12:24.277 --> 00:12:28.110
"gave the last full measure of devotion

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:30.537
in service of their country."

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:35.880
We are joined in humility, gratitude,

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:38.610
and our own measure of devotion,

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:41.640
knowing that as we seek to understand

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:44.190
an increasingly complex world,

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:47.310
we are sheltered by the vigilant service

00:12:47.310 --> 00:12:50.163
of citizens to our republic.

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:54.960
We ask special regards
for those who have served

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:59.960
but who have not yet been
found and returned home,

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:03.360
and for those who have returned

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:06.603
but who have yet to
find themselves at home.

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:10.350
We honor our military

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:14.070
and ask that whatever is required of them,

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:17.430
be informed by your infinite wisdom,

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:19.500
the light and truth,

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:22.500
as they honorably and loyally seek

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:27.330
to create a peaceful and
secure future for us all.

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:28.163
Amen.

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:47.340
- Good afternoon, President
Salovey, Secretary Goff-Crews,

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:51.990
Provost Strobel, Director
Bajwa, Rod Lowe, Dean Sodi,

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:54.660
the Veterans Day Committee,
distinguished guests,

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:57.300
family and friends,
members of our community,

00:13:57.300 --> 00:13:59.550
and of course our veterans.

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:00.900
To everyone here today,

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:03.660
thank you for coming together
to celebrate those who,

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:05.400
during times of peace and war,

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:08.643
swore to defend our nation and
its most sacred principles.

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:11.310
It's a privilege to be with you all today

00:14:11.310 --> 00:14:13.440
at an event hosted by a university

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:17.250
with such a long standing and
storied tradition of service.

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:20.070
Only a few moments' walk
away stands a statue

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:23.250
of one of this nation's
earliest and finest patriots,

00:14:23.250 --> 00:14:25.320
placed adjacent to the
building he called home

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:27.390
while a student here at Yale.

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:29.354
Nathan Hale's famous last words,

00:14:29.354 --> 00:14:30.660
that his only regret

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:33.180
is he had but one life
to lose for his country,

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:36.570
embodies the same principle
that is held by every person

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:38.283
who has worn our military's cloth.

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:41.340
If you speak to a hundred
different veterans,

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:43.050
you'll hear a hundred different stories,

00:14:43.050 --> 00:14:46.080
but one principle is universally true:

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:48.480
each and every one of them
pledged to serve a cause

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:51.870
far greater than self at
great personal expense

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:55.380
and without any assurance
of what the future may hold.

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:57.240
I'm continually humbled and inspired

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:58.680
by those with whom I served

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:02.070
and by the veterans I've met
since hanging up my uniform,

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.750
and I want to thank everyone here today

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:05.910
who has worn, currently wears,

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:08.193
or one day will wear our nation's uniform.

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:12.210
My story with the
military, like many others,

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:15.390
does not begin with a family
tradition of martial service.

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:16.943
I never really thought
about joining the military

00:15:16.943 --> 00:15:19.590
until my senior year of high school.

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:22.620
At the time, all I knew was
that I had to get away from home

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:26.190
and the military seemed like
a good opportunity to do so.

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:27.120
The determining factor

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:30.360
of why I chose to enlist in the
Marines was, in all honesty,

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:32.252
that I like their uniforms the best,

00:15:32.252 --> 00:15:34.050
(audience laughing)

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:35.700
which I suppose is somewhat ironic

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:37.740
since I didn't know the
Marines had flight suits

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:39.829
and that's the uniform I
ended up wearing the most.

00:15:39.829 --> 00:15:42.120
(audience laughing)

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:43.620
And so I shipped off to bootcamp

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:45.840
shortly after my high school graduation,

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:48.420
not sure of what would become of me.

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:50.400
I'll never forget that
first night in bootcamp.

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:52.140
I remember thinking repeatedly

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:54.440
that I had made the worst
decision of my life.

00:15:55.500 --> 00:15:57.600
But as you quickly learn in the military,

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:00.270
you must adapt to new
situations and challenges

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:02.790
and excel in stressful environments,

00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:03.900
whether it's making your bed

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:06.210
with a perfect 45 degree
fold in the corner

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:08.550
while a drill instructor
screams in your face,

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:11.460
or calling an Osprey to the
deck at night in heavy dust

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:13.890
while the enemy lurks in the darkness.

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:16.440
And so my initial apprehension gave way,

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:18.630
and as the Marine Corps
became my new home,

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:19.590
I realized that I had made

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:21.903
the greatest possible
decision by enlisting.

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:24.780
While I look back fondly on many moments

00:16:24.780 --> 00:16:26.730
with some of the greatest
people I'll ever know,

00:16:26.730 --> 00:16:28.590
my time in the military also created

00:16:28.590 --> 00:16:30.240
some of my greatest struggles,

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:31.500
and leaving those in the past

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:33.663
at one point seemed insurmountable.

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:35.880
Returning home from deployment

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:38.340
was one of the hardest
things I've ever had to do.

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:41.130
Deployment itself was challenging enough.

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:42.900
It was hard to start each day with a brief

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:44.520
on the number of enemy killed in action

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:46.230
over the previous 24 hours

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:48.510
and the weapon systems we
were expected to encounter

00:16:48.510 --> 00:16:50.070
throughout our flight,

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:52.380
to navigate through airbursts
and wonder if the next one

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:54.330
would be the one that took us down,

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:56.250
to see cities destroyed by airstrikes

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:58.110
and the ravages of war,

00:16:58.110 --> 00:17:00.060
to point a machine gun
at another human being

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:01.590
and have to decide if he was the enemy

00:17:01.590 --> 00:17:03.660
hiding within a civilian population

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:05.970
or an innocent bystander
caught in the middle of a war

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:07.023
he didn't ask for.

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:09.480
But it was also hard to return from that

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:11.730
to a place that no longer felt like home,

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:13.770
to go about each day
feeling like a part of me

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:15.660
was lost in the desert.

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:16.950
It was hard to leave behind a place

00:17:16.950 --> 00:17:18.750
where I felt that
everything I did mattered,

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:22.380
not knowing if I'd ever have
that purpose in my life again.

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:24.870
It was harder still to
leave behind the military

00:17:24.870 --> 00:17:26.640
along with the people
who were there with me

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:28.170
throughout it all.

00:17:28.170 --> 00:17:30.120
For several years after
leaving the Marines,

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:32.610
I didn't want anything
to do with the military.

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:34.650
I avoided reaching out
to those I served with

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:36.960
and I did everything I
could to reclaim who I was

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:39.273
and leave the military
forever in the past.

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:42.330
But that's easier said than done.

00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:44.700
My non-commissioned officer blood stripes

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:45.690
were beat into my legs,

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:48.480
and my sergeant chevrons
were pounded on my chest.

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:51.420
I fought for my wings
in the skies over Iraq,

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:54.480
and I earned the right to wear
the eagle, globe, and anchor.

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:56.190
These things are not given lightly,

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:59.010
and once given nothing can take them away.

00:17:59.010 --> 00:18:00.687
I'm grateful that
they'll always be with me

00:18:00.687 --> 00:18:02.400
and I no longer lament the part of me

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:04.230
that never returned from the desert

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:06.270
or avoid the memories
of my time in uniform

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:08.493
because those things
made me who I am today.

00:18:09.519 --> 00:18:11.010
I'm forever grateful

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:12.960
for those who helped
me along this journey.

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:15.240
Professor Jeannie Johnson
for helping me find a path

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:16.920
when I left the Marines

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:20.100
and for being a friend and
mentor for nearly half a decade.

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:21.810
Rose McCard for helping me navigate

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:23.460
service after the military

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:25.020
and for growing my love of foreign policy

00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:27.390
and international relations.

00:18:27.390 --> 00:18:28.410
Jeannie was my professor

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:30.570
during my first semester of college.

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:32.880
While I certainly felt out of place

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:35.850
in our freshman introductory
international relations course

00:18:35.850 --> 00:18:38.520
as a former Marine sergeant
with a disheveled beard

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:41.310
who had barely graduated high school,

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:42.660
Jeannie recognized that I understood

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:44.610
the importance of the
material we were learning

00:18:44.610 --> 00:18:47.040
because I had lived through
international relations

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:49.530
at its most visceral level in Iraq.

00:18:49.530 --> 00:18:51.090
She immediately took me under her wing

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:53.850
and has supported me
constantly ever since.

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:56.250
Rose was my supervisor
at the State Department.

00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:58.410
As an army officer who
joined the State Department

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:00.450
after transitioning from active duty,

00:19:00.450 --> 00:19:02.190
Rose quickly became an inspiration to me

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:05.430
as I sought to regain a
sense of purpose in life.

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:06.960
Because of my experience in the Marines

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:08.220
and Rose's belief in me,

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:09.690
I was able to work on a project

00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:11.880
whose effects are right
now being used by Ukraine

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:13.710
to defend its independence,

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:15.420
to represent State Department equities

00:19:15.420 --> 00:19:17.310
to the National Security Council,

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:18.780
and to help formulate policy

00:19:18.780 --> 00:19:21.090
as crisises develop throughout the world.

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:22.350
Because of Jeannie and Rose,

00:19:22.350 --> 00:19:24.630
I found my post-military purpose in life

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:25.863
in foreign relations.

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:28.140
But Jeannie and Rose's support for me

00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:30.180
highlights something far more profound:

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:31.860
veterans bring something to the table

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:34.500
that is impossible to find anywhere else.

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:36.000
Veterans have real world experience

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.640
that is gained in the most
trying of circumstances,

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:40.590
skillsets that are
developed in furtherance

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:42.210
of the most serious tasks,

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:43.590
and an ability to succeed

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:45.440
in the most challenging environments.

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:49.530
I'm grateful for the incredible
faculty at Yale Law School

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:51.060
for recognizing this.

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:52.560
At the start of her term,

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:55.290
Dean Gerken immediately set
to work supporting veterans.

00:19:55.290 --> 00:19:56.430
And thanks to her efforts,

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:59.370
Yale Law School isn't just
the best place to study law,

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:02.477
but is a place that veterans
can truly call home.

00:20:02.477 --> 00:20:03.870
One of the greatest testaments

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:05.370
to Yale Law's commitment to veterans

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:07.260
is the Veterans Legal Services Clinic,

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:08.700
which under the leadership

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:11.730
of Mike Wishnie, Meghan
Brooks, and Jason Parkins

00:20:11.730 --> 00:20:15.090
is a significant engine of
improvement to veterans' benefits

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:16.020
and has changed the lives

00:20:16.020 --> 00:20:18.090
of countless veterans for the better.

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:20.280
It's telling that every semester

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:23.280
there's overwhelming student
interest to work in the clinic.

00:20:24.510 --> 00:20:27.240
I also wouldn't be here today
without my family's support.

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:28.980
It can't be easy for any parent

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:30.870
to watch their child ship off to war,

00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:32.850
and I can't imagine the
burdens my parents felt

00:20:32.850 --> 00:20:34.560
knowing they signed my contract for me

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:37.320
when I was too young to enlist on my own.

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:39.360
Our military families deserve nothing

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:42.960
but our nation's highest
respect and gratitude.

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:44.670
They hold together families on their own

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:46.320
for months and years.

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:48.840
They say goodbye to
parents, siblings, children,

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:50.190
and significant others,

00:20:50.190 --> 00:20:52.440
and live through periods
of incredible stress,

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:55.500
limited communication, and immense worry.

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:57.300
They often put their lives on hold

00:20:57.300 --> 00:21:00.210
and move to places far
from familiar faces.

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:01.920
Nothing we could do could ever repay

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:04.410
the sacrifices you all have made.

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:05.400
And of course, I'm grateful

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:07.350
for those who are no longer with us,

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:10.980
whose sacrifices are the reason
we can be together today.

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:12.720
At many Marine Corps ceremonies,

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:15.750
there's an empty place set
at a table for the fallen.

00:21:15.750 --> 00:21:18.120
While at first, this table to
me was just a solemn reminder

00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:20.700
of the nature of military service,

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:21.930
as the years have passed,

00:21:21.930 --> 00:21:24.960
more and more friends have
come to occupy that empty seat.

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:26.790
You live with me every day

00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:29.340
and I'm forever thankful
for our memories together.

00:21:30.420 --> 00:21:32.010
While I once ignored this day,

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:34.500
Veterans Day is now a
day that I take pride

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:35.820
in the best parts of my service

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:38.040
and try and heal from the difficult ones,

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:39.660
smile back on fond memories,

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:40.920
and express gratitude

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:43.530
for the challenges that
the military imposed,

00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:45.450
cherish those who are still with us,

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:47.160
and honor those who are gone,

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:48.780
knowing that my time in the military

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:51.840
has given me the tools to
overcome the insurmountable,

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:54.660
and I'll always find inspiration
in my brothers and sisters

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:56.403
as they navigate their own paths.

00:21:57.540 --> 00:21:59.370
To my fellow members of the Armed Forces,

00:21:59.370 --> 00:22:01.440
however you choose to spend Veterans Day,

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:03.990
I'm grateful to be here
with you all today.

00:22:03.990 --> 00:22:05.730
Thank you for answering the call.

00:22:05.730 --> 00:22:07.200
Wherever you may be in your journey,

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:09.150
whether you're a member of our ROTC

00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:11.670
who's training to take
charge of your first command,

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:13.650
you left the military decades ago,

00:22:13.650 --> 00:22:15.720
or you fall somewhere in between,

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:18.450
I hope the future brings
you nothing but joy,

00:22:18.450 --> 00:22:19.950
and know that your brothers and sisters

00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:21.603
are rooting for your success.

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:25.350
Thank you for taking the time
today to honor our veterans.

00:22:25.350 --> 00:22:26.550
May we honor them every day

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:29.280
by enjoying the blessings of
this nation that they served.

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:30.242
Thank you.

00:22:30.242 --> 00:22:33.409
(audience applauding)

00:22:51.876 --> 00:22:54.459
(somber music)

00:22:56.281 --> 00:22:58.364
- [Soldier] To attention!

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:29.657
(somber music continues)

00:23:44.622 --> 00:23:45.955
All right, rest!

00:23:47.727 --> 00:23:49.560
- [Soldier] Order. Go!

00:24:02.107 --> 00:24:03.603
- She's okay, she fainted.

00:24:03.603 --> 00:24:05.843
She's okay, she's fine, (indistinct).

00:24:05.843 --> 00:24:07.370
- Okay. All right.
- You should tell them.

00:24:07.370 --> 00:24:10.560
- So just so you know
that our student is okay.

00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:12.300
She's talking. She just fainted.

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:14.910
It is a little bit hot
in here, I have to say.

00:24:14.910 --> 00:24:16.650
I don't think they were
expecting to be here that long,

00:24:16.650 --> 00:24:19.863
but she's fine and we will
continue with our program, okay?

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:22.440
And actually, why don't we
give a round of applause

00:24:22.440 --> 00:24:23.358
to our students, by the way?

00:24:23.358 --> 00:24:28.358
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:24:35.430 --> 00:24:37.140
And Michael, I wanna tell you,

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:39.480
your comments were really quite wonderful,

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:41.970
so we are really grateful that
you shared your story with us

00:24:41.970 --> 00:24:43.350
and that you chose to come to Yale,

00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:44.727
so welcome and thank you.

00:24:44.727 --> 00:24:47.894
(audience applauding)

00:24:53.169 --> 00:24:55.050
Now, one of the things
that was really poignant

00:24:55.050 --> 00:24:56.580
about what Michael was talking about

00:24:56.580 --> 00:24:59.820
was the need to have people in your sphere

00:24:59.820 --> 00:25:02.280
who support you as a
veteran wherever you may be,

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:03.420
including at Yale.

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:05.790
And right now we want to, as a university,

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:08.880
recognize someone who has
made outstanding contributions

00:25:08.880 --> 00:25:12.213
to veterans with the
Veterans Day Tribute Award.

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:15.750
This year, it is my great
honor to present the award

00:25:15.750 --> 00:25:18.540
to Risa Sodi, the Assistant Dean

00:25:18.540 --> 00:25:21.295
and Director of Academic
Advising and Special Programs

00:25:21.295 --> 00:25:23.640
in Yale College.

00:25:23.640 --> 00:25:24.473
Risa.

00:25:24.473 --> 00:25:29.402
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:25:37.975 --> 00:25:39.720
Now, many of us may know Risa

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:43.530
because she has been at the
university for 25 years,

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:46.020
but for the last nine years,

00:25:46.020 --> 00:25:49.560
she has been the Director
of the Eli Whitney Program,

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:51.060
The Eli Whitney Student Program,

00:25:51.060 --> 00:25:53.100
and the Transfer Student Programs,

00:25:53.100 --> 00:25:55.530
and most of our Yale
College student veterans

00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:57.990
find their way to Yale and to New Haven

00:25:57.990 --> 00:25:59.343
through those two programs.

00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:03.210
In 2013, there were six student veterans

00:26:03.210 --> 00:26:05.130
in the Eli Whitney Program,

00:26:05.130 --> 00:26:08.070
but today there are 36,

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:10.440
and Dean Sodi continues to provide

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:13.980
the same individualized and
loving attention and support

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:17.853
to each Eli Whitney student
with grace and generosity.

00:26:18.840 --> 00:26:20.820
Her support has made countless students

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:22.503
feel as if they belong at Yale.

00:26:23.340 --> 00:26:26.640
For example, former Eli Whitney student,

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:28.110
Yale College graduate,

00:26:28.110 --> 00:26:30.900
and current Yale Law School
student Hillary Browning

00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:33.720
recalls feeling really cared for

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:36.510
the moment she met Dean Sodi.

00:26:36.510 --> 00:26:38.520
In Hillary's time at Yale College,

00:26:38.520 --> 00:26:41.190
Dean Sodi provided not
only academic support,

00:26:41.190 --> 00:26:42.990
but also personal guidance,

00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:45.243
which she said really
helped her to succeed.

00:26:46.500 --> 00:26:49.230
But in addition to her
supports of student,

00:26:49.230 --> 00:26:51.270
student veterans, and the like,

00:26:51.270 --> 00:26:55.290
she has also served on the
Veterans Day Ceremony Committee

00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:56.700
for many, many years.

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:59.100
And so her dedication
and support to veterans,

00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:01.020
to Yale's veterans in particular,

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:04.140
are recognized today for the service

00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:05.700
that goes beyond the student community

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:07.743
to the entire veterans community.

00:27:09.060 --> 00:27:10.560
So Risa, I am really pleased

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:13.470
to present to you today this award,

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:15.300
if you'd come and join me,

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:17.310
on behalf of the Veterans Day Committee,

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:18.960
in recognition of your support

00:27:18.960 --> 00:27:20.910
for all of our veterans at Yale.

00:27:20.910 --> 00:27:23.220
Please join me in welcoming her

00:27:23.220 --> 00:27:26.132
and also sharing our admiration
and respect for her service.

00:27:26.132 --> 00:27:31.132
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:27:50.842 --> 00:27:54.210
Now, I just saw that Senator
Blumenthal has joined us

00:27:54.210 --> 00:27:55.260
and I want to make sure

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:56.730
if you have time to make some comments,

00:27:56.730 --> 00:27:57.750
that would be wonderful for us.

00:27:57.750 --> 00:27:58.770
Welcome.

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:01.036
Another alum as well, so thank you.

00:28:01.036 --> 00:28:04.156
(audience applauding)

00:28:04.156 --> 00:28:05.156
- Thank you.

00:28:07.260 --> 00:28:09.330
Thank you, congratulations, Dean Sodi,

00:28:09.330 --> 00:28:14.130
and congratulations to Yale.

00:28:14.130 --> 00:28:17.790
Very proud to be a graduate
of the law school here,

00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:20.610
as are two of my children,

00:28:20.610 --> 00:28:24.813
and another of my children
a graduate of the college.

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:27.330
And over the years,

00:28:27.330 --> 00:28:31.690
I have seen Yale

00:28:32.700 --> 00:28:35.500
become more and more

00:28:37.410 --> 00:28:40.020
respectful, I think, is the right word,

00:28:40.020 --> 00:28:43.320
of our veterans since
the days when I came here

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:45.280
as a law student shortly after

00:28:46.980 --> 00:28:48.063
the Vietnam War.

00:28:49.140 --> 00:28:51.690
And Yale has been at the forefront

00:28:51.690 --> 00:28:54.480
of recognizing the immense contributions

00:28:54.480 --> 00:28:55.660
made by our veterans

00:28:57.810 --> 00:28:59.910
in a way that I think has provided a model

00:28:59.910 --> 00:29:02.910
for other educational institutions

00:29:02.910 --> 00:29:05.610
as I've seen them around the country.

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:07.830
I serve on the Veterans Affairs Committee

00:29:07.830 --> 00:29:09.980
as well as on the Armed
Services Committee,

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:15.390
and I think the pattern
in our great nation,

00:29:15.390 --> 00:29:16.800
the greatest in the history of the world,

00:29:16.800 --> 00:29:21.063
is that all too often we fail.

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:23.590
We utterly fail

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:29.490
to address the needs of our veterans,

00:29:29.490 --> 00:29:31.500
give them what they deserve,

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:32.943
when they most need it,

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:36.423
in the aftermath of their service.

00:29:37.590 --> 00:29:41.550
And that is true of the
post-9/11 generation

00:29:41.550 --> 00:29:42.480
of veterans as well.

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:45.550
Only literally this Congress

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:50.493
did we succeed in passing a measure,

00:29:51.630 --> 00:29:52.560
it's called the PACT Act,

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:54.570
that will provide care and benefits

00:29:54.570 --> 00:29:59.163
for veterans exposed to
toxic chemicals, poisons,

00:30:00.270 --> 00:30:02.673
burn pits on the battlefield.

00:30:03.870 --> 00:30:08.870
3.5 million veterans.
(audience applauding)

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:13.740
But it took years to do it.

00:30:13.740 --> 00:30:16.230
I started working on it
a number of years ago.

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:17.763
It was bipartisan.

00:30:19.170 --> 00:30:23.190
But the VA actually
opposed us in this effort,

00:30:23.190 --> 00:30:24.330
just as it opposed us

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:27.210
when we sought to get care and benefits

00:30:27.210 --> 00:30:30.480
for veterans who suffered

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:32.500
as a result of Agent Orange exposure

00:30:33.420 --> 00:30:34.860
a generation before.

00:30:34.860 --> 00:30:37.740
And it took literally a generation

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:38.823
to get that coverage.

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:43.440
And now the PACT Act will provide

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:45.990
the presumption of service connection

00:30:45.990 --> 00:30:48.180
when people develop

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:53.180
23 different kinds of cancers
or liver disease, emphysema,

00:30:54.030 --> 00:30:57.420
all of the hideous
diseases that result from

00:30:57.420 --> 00:31:01.923
being in the way of these toxic chemicals.

00:31:03.540 --> 00:31:06.810
If burn pits were located

00:31:06.810 --> 00:31:10.380
anywhere around Yale

00:31:10.380 --> 00:31:12.540
or around New Haven or
anywhere in Connecticut,

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:13.470
anywhere in the United States,

00:31:13.470 --> 00:31:16.830
they would be a scandal,
there would be mass protests.

00:31:16.830 --> 00:31:18.750
But our military men and women

00:31:18.750 --> 00:31:23.010
were exposed to these toxic
chemicals day after day,

00:31:23.010 --> 00:31:27.600
and I am sure some of
the veterans here today

00:31:27.600 --> 00:31:31.323
know people who have
suffered from those diseases.

00:31:33.780 --> 00:31:37.210
So I think one of the lessons is that

00:31:38.190 --> 00:31:42.333
honoring our veterans
this day, fine to do.

00:31:43.350 --> 00:31:45.300
I'm proud of our state

00:31:45.300 --> 00:31:47.580
for having numerous ceremonies,

00:31:47.580 --> 00:31:49.630
and I've been to several of them already.

00:31:51.270 --> 00:31:55.263
Rhetoric, words, are good,

00:31:56.100 --> 00:31:59.043
but even better and even more important,

00:32:00.330 --> 00:32:01.260
and our obligation,

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:05.673
is to honor veterans in deed
and action, not just in word.

00:32:07.350 --> 00:32:09.730
If you know a veteran who has

00:32:11.580 --> 00:32:15.000
been in the military and deployed,

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:16.800
as two of my sons have,

00:32:16.800 --> 00:32:19.920
during these 20 years of war,

00:32:19.920 --> 00:32:21.320
make sure they get screened.

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:25.290
On just this last Tuesday,

00:32:25.290 --> 00:32:27.873
every one of them is
eligible for screening.

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:32.400
Make sure they get screened

00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:35.350
because cancers like glioblastoma

00:32:36.870 --> 00:32:41.100
or more to the point kidney/liver diseases

00:32:41.100 --> 00:32:42.513
can be caught early.

00:32:44.040 --> 00:32:48.540
And we need to invest in
education, skill training,

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:50.313
healthcare for our veterans,

00:32:51.510 --> 00:32:53.290
not only the physical wounds of war

00:32:54.570 --> 00:32:55.920
that are apparent when people leave,

00:32:55.920 --> 00:32:57.960
but also the invisible wounds of war

00:32:57.960 --> 00:33:02.100
like these cancers that don't
manifest until years after,

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:04.240
or PTS

00:33:05.520 --> 00:33:09.603
and other mental health issues.

00:33:11.610 --> 00:33:14.080
Thank you for what you'll do

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:17.463
to make sure that our
veterans are taken care of.

00:33:18.330 --> 00:33:20.400
We're very fortunate to
have here in West Haven

00:33:20.400 --> 00:33:22.900
one of the best VA hospitals
in the whole country.

00:33:23.940 --> 00:33:26.490
It needs more investment as well.

00:33:26.490 --> 00:33:28.803
Again, action, not just word.

00:33:29.670 --> 00:33:31.620
And thank you to Yale,

00:33:31.620 --> 00:33:33.960
thank you to everyone who is here today,

00:33:33.960 --> 00:33:36.960
for keeping faith with our
veterans and honoring them

00:33:36.960 --> 00:33:38.160
as you're doing today.

00:33:38.160 --> 00:33:39.672
Thank you very, very much.

00:33:39.672 --> 00:33:42.839
(audience applauding)

00:33:52.184 --> 00:33:54.810
- All right, are you ready
for a military salute?

00:33:54.810 --> 00:33:56.700
All right, let's get going.

00:33:56.700 --> 00:33:58.230
This is almost like the Harvard-Yale game,

00:33:58.230 --> 00:34:00.830
but this is like, so we're
gonna really hear it now.

00:34:07.671 --> 00:34:10.504
(patriotic music)

00:34:18.777 --> 00:34:21.944
(audience applauding)

00:34:37.706 --> 00:34:41.373
(patriotic music continues)

00:35:07.720 --> 00:35:11.387
(patriotic music continues)

00:35:28.211 --> 00:35:31.489
(audience applauding)

00:35:31.489 --> 00:35:33.169
♪ Off we go ♪

00:35:33.169 --> 00:35:35.449
♪ Into the wild blue yonder ♪

00:35:35.449 --> 00:35:39.329
♪ Climbing high into the sun ♪

00:35:39.329 --> 00:35:40.809
♪ Here they come ♪

00:35:40.809 --> 00:35:43.169
♪ Zooming to meet our thunder ♪

00:35:43.169 --> 00:35:45.689
♪ At 'em now, give 'em the gun ♪

00:35:45.689 --> 00:35:47.328
♪ Give em the gun, hey ♪

00:35:47.328 --> 00:35:48.688
♪ Down we dive ♪

00:35:48.688 --> 00:35:51.048
♪ Spouting our flame from under ♪

00:35:51.048 --> 00:35:54.808
♪ Off with one helluva roar ♪

00:35:54.808 --> 00:35:56.928
♪ We live in fame ♪

00:35:56.928 --> 00:35:58.968
♪ Or go down in flame, hey ♪

00:35:58.968 --> 00:36:03.215
♪ Nothing'll stop the U.S. Air Force ♪

00:36:03.215 --> 00:36:06.048
(patriotic music)

00:36:09.154 --> 00:36:12.554
♪ From the halls of Montezuma ♪

00:36:12.554 --> 00:36:15.434
♪ To the shores of Tripoli ♪

00:36:15.434 --> 00:36:16.634
(audience applauding)

00:36:16.634 --> 00:36:20.634
♪ We will fight our country's battles ♪

00:36:20.634 --> 00:36:24.634
♪ In the air, on land, and sea ♪

00:36:24.634 --> 00:36:25.674
♪ First to fight ♪

00:36:25.674 --> 00:36:28.674
♪ For right and freedom ♪

00:36:28.674 --> 00:36:32.674
♪ And to keep our honor clean ♪

00:36:32.674 --> 00:36:36.754
♪ We are proud to claim the title ♪

00:36:36.754 --> 00:36:40.337
♪ Of United States Marines ♪

00:36:41.634 --> 00:36:43.292
(patriotic music)

00:36:43.292 --> 00:36:45.213
♪ Stand, Navy, out to sea ♪

00:36:45.213 --> 00:36:49.013
♪ Fight our battle cry ♪

00:36:49.013 --> 00:36:52.413
♪ We'll never change our course ♪

00:36:52.413 --> 00:36:56.893
♪ So vicious foe steer shy ♪

00:36:56.893 --> 00:37:00.293
♪ Roll out the TNT ♪

00:37:00.293 --> 00:37:04.493
♪ Oh, anchors aweigh ♪

00:37:04.493 --> 00:37:07.812
♪ Sail on to victory ♪

00:37:07.812 --> 00:37:11.773
♪ And sink their bones
to Davy Jones, hooray ♪

00:37:11.773 --> 00:37:13.933
(patriotic music)

00:37:13.933 --> 00:37:18.933
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:37:20.891 --> 00:37:24.558
(patriotic music continues)

00:37:34.789 --> 00:37:38.622
(people indistinctly singing)

00:37:50.340 --> 00:37:55.340
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:38:09.027 --> 00:38:09.960
- Let us go forth

00:38:09.960 --> 00:38:12.051
from this place of profound
memory and deep meaning

00:38:12.051 --> 00:38:14.010
with grateful hearts,

00:38:14.010 --> 00:38:16.800
grateful for the service and
sacrifice of our veterans

00:38:16.800 --> 00:38:18.360
and their families.

00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:20.220
Let us never forget
all that they have done

00:38:20.220 --> 00:38:23.370
to bravely protect
freedom and human rights.

00:38:23.370 --> 00:38:26.270
From the moment our veterans
made the commitment to serve,

00:38:27.450 --> 00:38:28.470
they were committing to a life

00:38:28.470 --> 00:38:29.970
that would be forever changed.

00:38:31.200 --> 00:38:33.450
May we be ever mindful
that the life of a veteran

00:38:33.450 --> 00:38:35.040
is always multidimensional

00:38:35.040 --> 00:38:37.560
and is rooted in the pride
and promise of liberation,

00:38:37.560 --> 00:38:40.170
coupled with the ever present possibility

00:38:40.170 --> 00:38:42.420
of peril or alienation.

00:38:42.420 --> 00:38:44.280
All of these things are held in tension

00:38:44.280 --> 00:38:46.870
and contain a longing for
a certain kind of peace

00:38:48.540 --> 00:38:50.043
to revive our weary world.

00:38:51.150 --> 00:38:52.470
As we honor our veterans

00:38:52.470 --> 00:38:55.050
and we seek to grow closer
to this abiding peace,

00:38:55.050 --> 00:38:56.580
may we come to know

00:38:56.580 --> 00:38:59.130
a peace born from wisdom
and understanding,

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:02.430
a peace nurtured with
compassion and mercy,

00:39:02.430 --> 00:39:05.250
a peace strengthened by
courage and commitment.

00:39:05.250 --> 00:39:08.970
And may we all be blessed
this day and every day

00:39:08.970 --> 00:39:11.640
by the shimmering light
of our God of many names,

00:39:11.640 --> 00:39:15.930
our God who creates, sustains,
and embraces all life.

00:39:15.930 --> 00:39:17.405
Amen.

00:39:17.405 --> 00:39:20.572
(audience applauding)

00:39:23.400 --> 00:39:25.050
- So that is our ceremony,

00:39:25.050 --> 00:39:26.943
and I wanna thank you all for coming.

00:39:28.200 --> 00:39:29.670
Many thanks to the Planning Committee

00:39:29.670 --> 00:39:34.260
and particularly our team
in Student Life Office,

00:39:34.260 --> 00:39:35.130
the Secretary's Office,

00:39:35.130 --> 00:39:37.290
who I know the military's prepared

00:39:37.290 --> 00:39:38.640
to make changes on a dime,

00:39:38.640 --> 00:39:39.720
but they have done pretty well,

00:39:39.720 --> 00:39:40.890
so I wanna thank you very much

00:39:40.890 --> 00:39:43.603
for changing everything quickly.

00:39:43.603 --> 00:39:46.770
(audience applauding)

00:39:47.700 --> 00:39:49.560
To our participants in our program,

00:39:49.560 --> 00:39:51.330
thank all of you for being
on the stage with us.

00:39:51.330 --> 00:39:52.890
And I also wanna give a special shout-out

00:39:52.890 --> 00:39:57.846
to the Yale School of Music
Brass Ensemble, brilliant.

00:39:57.846 --> 00:39:59.560
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:39:59.560 --> 00:40:00.810
You're awesome.

00:40:06.930 --> 00:40:08.441
So as we close,

00:40:08.441 --> 00:40:12.030
I want us to remember
that when today has passed

00:40:12.030 --> 00:40:13.860
and when we return to our busy lives,

00:40:13.860 --> 00:40:15.810
and they are busy nowadays,

00:40:15.810 --> 00:40:19.050
I want us to remember
to continue to remember

00:40:19.050 --> 00:40:20.790
the men and women of Yale

00:40:20.790 --> 00:40:23.130
who have faithfully

00:40:23.130 --> 00:40:26.213
and honestly served all of us.

00:40:26.213 --> 00:40:28.710
They protected us, they've served us.

00:40:28.710 --> 00:40:31.380
We are a stronger and
more diverse community

00:40:31.380 --> 00:40:32.223
because of them.

00:40:33.120 --> 00:40:34.697
So thank you all for your service,

00:40:34.697 --> 00:40:37.261
and have a really, really good day.

00:40:37.261 --> 00:40:40.428
(audience applauding)

00:40:48.333 --> 00:40:50.916
(bright music)

